Sliding Window

Instead of checking all subarrays (❌ O(n²)), we maintain a window:

[left ........ right]


We:
Expand window → right += 1
Shrink window → left += 1
# --------------------------

When to Use Sliding Window
Use it when the problem asks for:
Subarray
Substring
Longest / Shortest
Sum / Count / Condition
# ----------------------------------

🔹 Type 1: Fixed Window Size

Window size = constant k
Example: Maximum sum of subarray of size k
Time: O(n)

window_sum = 0
left = 0

for right in range(len(arr)):
    window_sum += arr[right]
    
    if right - left + 1 == target:
        window_sum -= arr[left]
        left += 1

🔹 Type 2: Variable Window Size

Window size changes based on condition
Example: Longest substring without repeating characters

left = 0

for right in range(len(arr)):
    # expand window

    while(condition_not_met):
        # shrink window
        left+=1

# -----------------------------------------

Goal,Window Type,Strategy
Fixed Size k,Fixed,Use if right >= k - 1 to start sliding.
Max/Longest,Variable,"Expand right until invalid, then while shrink left."
Min/Smallest,Variable,"Expand right until valid, then while shrink left to optimize."

| Problem         | Time | Space |
| --------------- | ---- | ----- |
| Fixed window    | O(n) | O(1)  |
| Variable window | O(n) | O(k)  |

--------------------------------------------------

Why is sliding window better than brute force for subarray / substring problems?
Brute force recalculates every possible subarray, leading to O(n²) time.
Sliding window avoids repeated work by reusing previous computations.
We expand and shrink the window incrementally, reducing time complexity to O(n).

Simple explanation
Brute force:
Recalculate sum/substring again and again ❌


Sliding window:
Add one element → remove one element → reuse result ✅
